{"page_content": "XGBoost Notes\nPage 8\n11. Interview cheat sheet\nQ: Why is XGBoost called boosting?\nBecause it combines many weak learners sequentially, with each new learner aimed at correcting errors / gradients from\nthe current ensemble.\nQ: Why use learning rate?\nIt shrinks each new tree contribution, making the optimization more conservative and often improving generalization when\ncombined with more trees.\nQ: What do gamma, lambda, and alpha do?\nGamma penalizes adding splits; lambda is L2 regularization on leaf weights; alpha is L1 regularization on leaf weights.\nQ: Why use second-order information?\nIt gives both slope and curvature information about the loss, enabling a more informed local approximation when choosing\nleaf weights and evaluating splits.\nQ: Why can XGBoost overfit?\nBecause highly complex trees and too many boosting rounds can fit noise. Use depth limits, regularization, subsampling,\nshrinkage, and validation/early stopping.\nQ: XGBoost vs random forest?\nRandom forest averages many largely independent randomized trees; XGBoost builds trees sequentially to reduce the\ncurrent model error.\nQ: What matters most in practice?\nGood validation strategy, objective/metric alignment, leakage prevention, appropriate class-imbalance handling, and\nsensible regularization often matter more than blind hyperparameter search.\nOne-minute memory map\n Sequential trees\n      -> gradients + Hessians\n      -> optimize leaf weights\n      -> choose splits by regularized gain\n      -> shrink with learning_rate\n      -> repeat\n      -> validate / early stop\n      -> control complexity with depth + regularization + sampling\nFinal takeaway\nXGBoost is best remembered as regularized, second-order gradient-boosted decision trees implemented with\nstrong engineering optimizations. For most interview questions, explain the sequential correction idea first, then\nadd the gradient/Hessian and regularization details.\nScope note\nThese notes are designed for study and interview revision. Exact defaults and APIs can vary by XGBoost version, so verify\nversion-specific behavior against the installed package documentation before using production code.", "metadata": {"doc_id": "9ee9679a26811f53", "parent_id": "9ee9679a26811f53_parent_2", "parent_index": 2, "source": "data\\uploads\\68cf5a29a674_XGBoost_Notes.pdf", "filename": "68cf5a29a674_XGBoost_Notes.pdf", "chapter_or_section": "Section 3", "subject_name": "", "subject_domain": "", "token_estimate": 535}}